# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Critical Breaking Changes

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `ThreadAbortException` | **Never raised** — dead code |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| Manual `Thread` + `volatile bool` | Use `CancellationToken` + `Task` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass the token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                var item = GetNextItem(token);
                ProcessItem(item);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Replaces ThreadAbortException handling
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop cleanly — no ResetAbort needed
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Log warning — task did not complete within timeout.
            // There is no forced kill equivalent; fix GetNextItem to
            // honor the token instead.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls

**1. `GetNextItem()` must accept a `CancellationToken`.**
Without this, there's no way to interrupt a blocking call — the old `Thread.Abort()` brute-forced it, but that API is gone. If it wraps a `BlockingCollection<T>`, use `.Take(token)`. If it's I/O, use async overloads.

**2. There is no equivalent to forced thread termination.**
`Thread.Abort()` was always dangerous (could corrupt state mid-lock). .NET 8 removes it entirely. If you have code that truly can't be cancelled cooperatively, isolate it in a **separate process** and kill that process.

**3. `catch (ThreadAbortException)` becomes dead code.**
It silently compiles but never triggers. Replace with `OperationCanceledException`.

**4. `Thread.ResetAbort()` throws at runtime, not compile time.**
This is a silent runtime bomb — code that compiled fine will crash when hit.

**5. `volatile bool` is a weak cancellation pattern.**
It can't interrupt blocking calls (`Thread.Sleep`, `Stream.Read`, `Monitor.Wait`). `CancellationToken` integrates with all of these via `WaitHandle` and async APIs.

**6. If you need `IHostedService` (ASP.NET Core / Worker Service):**

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            var item = await GetNextItemAsync(stoppingToken);
            await ProcessItemAsync(item);
        }
    }
}
// Register: builder.Services.AddHostedService<BackgroundProcessor>();
```

This gives you automatic graceful shutdown integration with the host lifetime.
